gm-method: eliminating an unattributed CDP CPU-profile bucket (V8 sampler's catch-all self-time, e.g. '(program)') that survives real JS-side fixes -- test GC-pressure first via heap-growth-rate + frame-time-spike-shape (GC pauses show as discrete large spikes among an otherwise tight distribution, e.g. <1% of frames >2.5x median; a smooth p50/p90/p99 gradient with zero big-spike outliers rules GC out even with nonzero heap growth). Test driver/command-buffer overhead by reading renderer.info.render.calls INSIDE the same page.evaluate as the frame-timing loop (a separate delayed read is stale/zero). Rule out synchronous GPU stalls via codesearch of the actual hot-path files for blocking gl.readPixels/getBufferSubData/texSubImage2D -- a correctly-fixed occlusion pipeline uses only non-blocking gl.getQueryParameter(query, QUERY_RESULT_AVAILABLE) polling. The decisive tool is the browser verb's own profile/trace prefixes: profile-prefix's wall_vs_cpu.offcpu_us near 0 (cpu_self_us >= wall_us) proves the main thread is continuously on-CPU with no hidden async/IO wait; trace-prefix's CDP-Tracing by_category breakdown then attributes that on-CPU time -- if the gpu category's summed wall-time dominates compositor(cc)/display(viz) categories by an order of magnitude while draw-call count is already low (in the low hundreds, not deopt/volume-explicable), the unattributed bucket is the main thread blocked synchronously inside native WebGL driver calls (e.g. ANGLE's D3D11 translation layer on Windows) that the JS sampler cannot resolve to a call site -- inherent driver-level cost, not a further JS-reachable optimization, correctly reported as blockedBy:external rather than guess-fixed.